{"schema":"plectis_maths_universe_excerpts_v2","paper":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface","excerpts":[["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:finite-state-nogo","A finite-state restriction for the\nstated pulse family","<p><em> For a “balanced pulse” family at location\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> (radius <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo></math></span>, parameters\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>ρ</mi></math></span>, moving mass between\npositions <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></math></span> with the weighted total <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>+</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ρ</mi></math></span> of the two\ncoefficients fixed), if a predecessor state is constant across the whole\nfamily, then no function <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>decode</mi></mrow><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>State</mi></mrow><mo accent=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> can recover the parameter <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span> from <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>state</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></span>. The family has exactly <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> members,\nso the fan-out is unbounded in <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>.\nMore strongly, any finite set of states carrying an exact decoder for\nthe family has at least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>ρ</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>\nelements, unbounded in <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Scope.</strong> Excludes bounded-state encodings of\npre-<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi></math></span> history that must distinguish\nevery member of the displayed balanced-pulse family, whether the\ncoefficients are <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>φ</mi></math></span> or a\nMöbius-support indicator. Applying it to #249 or #257 requires showing\nthat the relevant orbit realises that family, and it does not rule out\nevery finite-state proof strategy. <strong>Object or\nrepresentation:</strong> about a <em>representation</em> class, the\nencodings of history that separate the balanced-pulse family; it says\nnothing about <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi></math></span> or <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span> directly.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:mobius-nogo","Möbius-support countermodel: the\nnatural negative-sign candidate overshoots","<p><em> The signed Lambert\nidentity <span class=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munder><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> is exact. Writing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>:=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>: <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> where <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>P</mi><mo>:=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>, and quantitatively <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>63</mn><mo>≤</mo><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></munder><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>d</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> (using the first positive\ntail term <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>μ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>) ; the negative-Möbius support\nstrictly <em>overshoots</em> <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> by\nat least <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>63</mn></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Scope.</strong> Rules out exactly one natural candidate\ninfinite Boolean support (the negative-Möbius set) as a witness for\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>. A\nroute-sufficiency no-go only ; it says nothing about whether <em>some\nother</em> infinite Boolean support sums to <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. <strong>Object or\nrepresentation:</strong> about the <em>object</em> ; a genuine value\ninequality for one specific candidate set, not a coordinate artifact.\nMargin <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>63</mn></math></span> is the concrete number\nany repair attempt (adding/removing finitely many elements) must close\nor exceed.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:finite-boolSupport-and-onesided","Finite computation and\nhalf-membership","<p><em> No finite positive-index Boolean support has\nvalue exactly <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>: the reduced\ndenominator of any finite Mersenne subset-sum is provably\n<strong>odd</strong> (each <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> is\nodd), while <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> needs an even\ndenominator. Separately, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝖢𝖾𝗋𝗍𝗂𝖿𝗂𝖾𝖽𝖦𝗋𝖾𝖾𝖽𝗒𝖬𝖾𝗋𝗌𝖾𝗇𝗇𝖾𝖣𝖾𝖺𝗍𝗁</mi></mrow></math></span> is\na finite-depth certificate of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>∉</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>, obtained by decidable\ntests on rational input. For example, the supplied source excludes <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></math></span> at level <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> with lookahead <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn></math></span>. Failure to find such a certificate at\na given depth does not establish membership. It records survival of that\nfinite test, not survival at every depth.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Scope.</strong> Any support representing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, if it exists, must be infinite. This\nfollows from denominator parity, independently of the certificate\nsearch. The search supplies a different fact: a successful exclusion\ncertificate proves nonmembership, whereas its absence in a bounded\nsearch does not prove membership or rationality. The same distinction\nmust be checked separately for any other certificate family.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:carry-survivor-extinction","Period exclusion for the totient\nseries","<p><em> For <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><munder><mo>∑</mo><mi>n</mi></munder><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>/</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></math></span>, the cited finite\ntest proves that <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>totientTail</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>totientTail</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>\nis not an integer by excluding every possible integer state in a bounded\nrange within finitely many steps. If the series were rational, some\npositive period <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> would make\nthese tail differences integral for every sufficiently large <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span>. Telescoping would then give\nintegrality also for every positive multiple <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>.</em></p>\n<p><em>Consequently, the required certificate supply is: for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> and every lower bound <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span>, there exist <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>m</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>N</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math></span> and a finite test length <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>K</mi></math></span> excluding integrality for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>m</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>. The unbounded choice of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span> is essential; a counterexample before\nthe unknown eventual threshold does not exclude an eventual period. It\nis sufficient instead to obtain such certificates for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>lcm</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> with both\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span> exceeding arbitrary prescribed lower\nbounds. The supplied finite theorem excludes the tail differences for\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>16</mn></math></span> at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>9</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, and hence excludes rational\nvalues whose reduced denominator divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>h</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for one of those <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>h</mi></math></span>. It does not establish the unbounded\ncertificate supply.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Scope.</strong> The Lean declarations used in this\ncomparison concern the totient series of Problem 249. Their carry\nrecurrences may suggest constructions for Problem 257, but applying them\nthere requires a new argument for the divisor transform of its chosen\nsupport. The totient-specific theorems do not supply that\nargument.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:scalar-localization","Denominator complement survives scaling","<p><em> For <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>:</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span>: if <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span> ;\ni.e. multiplying by the integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>\nshrinks the displayed denominator down <em>into</em> <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi></math></span> ; then the\n<strong>complementary</strong> denominator factor <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span> divides <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span>: </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mi>H</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo>∧</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>⋅</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow><mo>∣</mo><mi>H</mi><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟹</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi><mo>∣</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> Equivalently, writing <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>, we have <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>c</mi></math></span>, so <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span> is an integer and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mi>c</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>num</mi></mrow></math></span>. Here <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> follows from <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow></math></span>, since the reduced\ndenominator is positive.</em></p>\n<p><em><strong>Scope.</strong> This is elementary rational arithmetic,\nindependent of the problem. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, divisibility gives the size bound\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≥</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>. The\nnonzero condition is essential: <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>\nclears every denominator and satisfies the divisibility conclusion\nwithout any positive lower bound on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo></math></span>. The lemma therefore constrains\ndenominator clearing by a nonzero bounded multiplier, not arbitrary\nmultiplication. <strong>Object or representation:</strong> about a\n<em>representation</em> ; pure denominator bookkeeping for <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi></mrow></math></span>, with zero problem-specific\ncontent; the upstream primitive is the domain-neutral extraction this\nlemma builds on.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#cor:mersenne-height","Mersenne specialisation","<p><em> Let\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span> be a positive rational number and\nlet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> be integers. If <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>∣</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>num</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>natAbs</mi></mrow></math></span>\nand <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, then <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>⋅</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>den</mi></mrow></math></span> ; a numerator <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn></math></span>-power lower bound plus a Mersenne-scale\nupper bound on <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span> together force a\ndenominator lower bound, <em>without</em> introducing a global prefix\nLCM. To see the inequality directly, write <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></math></span> in lowest terms with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>. The hypotheses give <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo>≥</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></msup></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></math></span>. The conclusion follows by\nsubstitution. An application of Lemma <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#lem:scalar-localization\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:scalar-localization\">10.13</a> must in\naddition supply its denominator-divisibility assumptions.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:critical-band-index","Quantifier collapse: one critical\nindex suffices, not <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></math></span>","<p><em>\nLet <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> and assume\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>. Then the purely\ncombinatorial statement <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝖣𝗒𝖺𝖽𝗂𝖼𝖡𝖺𝗇𝖽𝖤𝗌𝖼𝖺𝗉𝖾</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝖢𝗋𝗂𝗍𝗂𝖼𝖺𝗅𝖣𝗒𝖺𝖽𝗂𝖼𝖡𝖺𝗇𝖽𝖨𝗇𝖽𝖾𝗑</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∧</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> collapses the <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span> band-avoidance\ncondition (formally <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>+</mo></mrow><mn>1</mn></math></span> separate\ninequalities) to checking exactly <em>one</em> nearest-boundary index\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>j</mi></math></span>. The range assumption ensures\nthat a dyadic threshold lies at or above <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi></math></span>. Choose the smallest such threshold:\nsmaller thresholds are already below <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi></math></span>, while larger thresholds have narrower\nforbidden bands. Without the range assumption all bands escape\nautomatically when <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>E</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span>,\nbut no critical index exists; <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> is the smallest example.\nSpecialised to the concrete seam reset charge, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mrow><mi>𝖲𝖾𝖺𝗆𝖴𝗉𝗉𝖾𝗋𝖱𝖾𝗌𝖾𝗍𝖢𝗋𝗂𝗍𝗂𝖼𝖺𝗅𝖡𝖺𝗇𝖽𝖤𝗌𝖼𝖺𝗉𝖾</mi></mrow></math></span>\nis proved logically <em>equivalent</em> to Definition <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#defn:band-escape\">10.15</a>’s hypothesis. Zero\nMersenne/seam content in the core lemma ; pure <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> arithmetic over powers of\n2.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:odometer","Divisor-residue form of the short-window\nphase","<p><em> For all <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>,\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext> </mtext><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mtext> </mtext><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mtable columnspacing=\"0em\" rowspacing=\"0.1em\"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>i</mi><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\"><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace></mstyle><mo>≡</mo><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\"><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mi>M</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></munder><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mtext> </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext> </mtext><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></munder><mtext> </mtext><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>⋅</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi><mstyle scriptlevel=\"0\"><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace></mstyle><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>d</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>∈</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></math></span> is the least <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></math></span> (so <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> depends only on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo lspace=\"0.278em\" rspace=\"0.278em\">mod</mo><mi>d</mi></math></span>), <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo>:=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>:</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>, and\nterms with <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>d</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span> are empty.\nIn particular, for every integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext> </mtext><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></munderover><mtext> </mtext><munderover><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></munderover><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em>\nand the right-hand side, with an arbitrary integer <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> in place of <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi></math></span>, depends on <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>x</mi></math></span> only through its residues modulo the\nintegers <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>D</mi></math></span>. The cutoff <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi></math></span> has to be fixed before the argument\nvaries: at <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> the cutoff\nis <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, the integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>3</mn></math></span> have the same residue modulo every\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>d</mi></math></span> with <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>2</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>, and yet <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> while <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Θ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:sqwitness","The terminal bound at square depths","<p><em> Let <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow></math></span> with\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>∉</mo><mi>A</mi></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Then for every <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><mi>ihc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>A</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo>:</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>𝚋𝚒𝚗𝚊𝚛𝚢𝙲𝚘𝚎𝚏𝚏𝚃𝚊𝚒𝚕</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mo>≤</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn><mtext> </mtext><mo>=</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>B</mi><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:squarefree","Squarefree support: a limit of the\ncertificate method, not an open value","<p><em> Let <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>:</mo><mi>n</mi><mtext> is squarefree</mtext><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span>. Then\n</em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> which is\nodd for every <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Consequently\nneither the digitwise nor the carry-aware divisibility-first\nblock-certificate schema has an instance at any even base.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:cpgs-equiv","Infinitely many positive skips are\nequivalent to half-membership","<p><em> Define </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>CPGS</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo>:=</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>c</mi><mtext> </mtext><mtext>skipped by the rational half-greedy orbit</mtext><mtext> </mtext><mo>∧</mo><mtext> </mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> The positivity\nconjunct is unconditionally true for every skipped <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>c</mi></math></span> (an odd-denominator parity fact), hence\ndeletable without changing the Prop’s meaning. What survives, <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mi>N</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"></mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>N</mi></math></span> skipped, is\nexactly <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝚐𝚛𝚎𝚎𝚍𝚢𝙼𝚎𝚛𝚜𝚎𝚗𝚗𝚎𝚂𝚔𝚒𝚙𝚙𝚎𝚍𝚂𝚞𝚙𝚙𝚘𝚛𝚝</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo><mrow><mi>Infinite</mi></mrow></math></span>,\nso </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>CPGS</mi></mrow><mtext> </mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">⟺</mo><mtext> </mtext><mrow><mi>HALF</mi></mrow><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:strip-equiv","Terminal carry bounds are\nequivalent to half-membership","<p><em> Consider finite sets <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>D</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mo>…</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></math></span> at arbitrarily\nlarge depths <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi></math></span> with </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mi>ihc</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">|</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>4.</mn></math></span></div>\n<p><em> This is the exact terminal-strip condition. It is\nequivalent to <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi>𝒜</mi></mrow></math></span>: the\nforward direction follows from the displayed finite-approximation\nestimate; for the converse, truncate an achieving support at <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math></span> and apply Lemma <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#lem:sqrt-witness\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#lem:sqrt-witness\">2.6</a>. Thus constant\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>4</mn></math></span> already suffices at cofinally\nmany depths. The relaxed constant <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mn>6</mn></math></span>\ngives a bound at every depth, using <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><mo>≤</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌊</mo><msqrt><mi>M</mi></msqrt><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">⌋</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, but that relaxation is not needed\nfor the cofinal statement.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:257-logarithmic-counterexample","arithmetic logarithmic counterexample","<p><em> For every integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>H</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span> and\nevery real <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, there are a\nsquarefree positive integer <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi></math></span> and a\nfinite nonempty set <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi></math></span> of distinct\nsquarefree positive integers such that </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span class=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mo movablelimits=\"true\">min</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>30</mn><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>H</mi></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"></mspace><msub><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mi>L</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>\n<p><em> For these <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></math></span>, some <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span> satisfies <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mrow><mi>𝒟</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></math></span>. Consequently, no\nabsolute constant <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi></math></span> satisfies </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable minlabelspacing=\"0em\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mtr><mtd><mtext>(</mtext><mtext>E</mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mtd><mtd><msub><mrow><mi>𝒟</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mrow><mn>𝟏</mn></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> uniformly in finite <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi></math></span>, positive integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>L</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi></math></span>, integers <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mi>R</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#cor:257-logarithmic-separation","finite-functional separation","<p><em>\nFor every finite nonempty <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi></math></span>, with\n<span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>lcm</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mtable minlabelspacing=\"0em\" displaystyle=\"true\"><mtr><mtd><mtext>(</mtext><mtext>20</mtext><mtext>)</mtext></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><munder><mo movablelimits=\"true\">max</mo><mrow><mi>ℓ</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></munder><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi></mrow><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo></mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>∣</mo><mi>ℓ</mi><mo>∣</mo><mi>N</mi><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></span></div>\n<p class=\"display-continue\"><em> where <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>N</mi></math></span> is\nuniform modulo <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>Q</mi></math></span>. The supports of\nTheorem <a class=\"math-ref\" href=\"papers/erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.html#thm:257-logarithmic-counterexample\" data-universe-ref=\"statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#thm:257-logarithmic-counterexample\">13.1</a>\nsatisfy <span class=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math></span> and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>30</mn><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></math></span>. In particular,\nno absolute <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>C</mi></math></span> gives <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><msub><mi>K</mi><mo>∗</mo></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mi>C</mi><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></math></span> for all finite\nnonempty <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi></math></span>, even after all covering\nsets, exponents, coefficients and positive weights have been\noptimised.</em></p>"],["statement:erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface#prop:257-logarithmic-initial-interval","ordinary initial intervals","<p><em>\nFor every finite nonempty <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>F</mi></math></span>,\ninteger <span class=\"math inline\"><math><mi>X</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, and <span\nclass=\"math inline\"><math><mn>0</mn><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>1</mn></math></span>, </em></p>\n<div class=\"math-display\"><span\nclass=\"math display\"><math display=\"block\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>X</mi></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">#</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>:</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">}</mo><mo>≤</mo><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><msub><mi>κ</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>.</mo></math></span></div>"]],"source":"erdos257-mersenne-reasoning-surface.json?v=09745a86500d"}
